WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
We want to thank Google's Science Journal App for supporting PBS Digital Studios.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.620
Imagine you had a time machine and with the
press of a button you could transport yourself

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.349
to your own 75th birthday.

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:15.450
Assuming you’re still around, what do you
hope to find yourself doing?

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.849
Writing your memoirs?

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:18.230
Sailing around the world?

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.210
Partying in San Junipero?

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.911
Very few of us would answer “cleaning toilets
at a fast-food joint,” or “begging for

00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:25.289
change on the street.”

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:30.050
No one wants their story to end that way,
yet shockingly few of us are taking the basic

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.640
steps to avoid it.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.460
The 
simple fact is that if we’re lucky to live

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:47.309
long enough, one day we will lose our desire
(or physical ability) to keep earning a paycheck.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:51.039
The older we get, the harder it becomes to
maintain a rigid work schedule.

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:55.640
And as modern medicine allows us to live longer
and longer, the time we expect to spend in

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.480
retirement might easily pass 30 years.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.700
Think of that for a second.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.809
30 years without income.

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:04.019
Nervous yet?

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.019
Good.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.409
So how much would you need to not spend those
years in abject poverty.

00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:13.680
Well, that depends on your personal needs,
standard of living, health issues, etc., but

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.750
as a starting point, the AARP recommends that
to replace a $40,000 per year income for 30

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:27.409
years, you’ll need to start your retirement
with--take a deep breath--$1.18 million.

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.360
If that number makes you feel a little dizzy…
well, you’re not alone.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:38.200
In one survey, Americans between 55 and 64
reported a median retirement savings of $120,000--only

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.590
10% of the amount advised by the AARP!

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:47.210
Another survey found that 75% of Americans
over 40 are behind saving for retirement and

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.810
28% over 55 have no retirement savings at
all!

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.799
There are many factors that contributed to
this problem.

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:57.170
For one thing, wage growth declined in the
70s and 80s.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:01.200
It picked back up in the 90s, but then the
housing boom convinced a lot of Americans

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.689
to go into debt to buy overpriced homes, and,
well, we know how that turned out.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:11.080
We’ve also seen an increase in cultural
pressure to show “visual displays of wealth.”

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.390
A study published in the Quarterly Journal
of Economics suggests that Americans are uniquely

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:20.690
concerned about seeming poor to others, so
they spend a disproportionate amount on things

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.890
like shoes, clothes and cars.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.090
It’s been great business for designer labels
and advertisers--not so much for our savings

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.230
accounts.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:34.000
Lastly, changes in government policies have
made it easier to not save money.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.680
In the past, employees were automatically
enrolled in “defined benefit plans” with

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.730
pre-set funding amounts to match their retirement
needs.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.650
Today’s workers have to “opt in” to
retirement plans like 401(k)s, and figure

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.810
out for themselves how much to set aside.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:54.700
Furthermore, these plans are often “leaky,”
meaning you’re allowed to remove funds prematurely,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.430
which makes it easy to steal from your own
retirement.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.390
Does all this mean that saving for retirement
is hopeless and you should just blow your

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.640
extra dough leasing a sports car?

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.640
No!

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.120
It’s still very possible to save up large
amounts of money on a modest income.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.260
The three special ingredients are Good Markets,
Compound Interest, and Time.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.970
To show you how these elements work together,
it’s time to…

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.060
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.040
Betty is 30 years old and makes $50,000/yr.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:31.160
She hasn’t saved a dime for retirement yet,
but this year she’s decided to start.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.420
Between the amount she is going to save into
her Roth IRA, her 401(k) at work, and her

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:42.530
401(k) match, she’s putting away $625 a
month, or $7,500 a year, That’s 15% of her

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.880
income--which many experts recommend as a
good savings target.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:53.520
At this rate, by the time she’s 65, Betty
will have personally deposited $262,500 into

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.220
her retirement account.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:00.450
Impressive, but still a long way from the
million dollars plus she’ll need to retire.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.740
But now we add our special ingredients!

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.820
Over the last 90 years, the stock market has
grown an average of 9.8% per year.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:14.320
But let’s assume a little less than that…
say, 7.5% If Betty can put together a decent

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.780
portfolio, she can expect her savings to grow
by an average of 7.5% per year.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:23.970
And as long as Betty doesn’t touch that
account, the dividends and interest she earns

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.130
will generate even more dividends and interest!

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:32.700
And over time, her savings doesn’t just
increase in a straight line… it increases

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.700
exponentially!

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:42.900
Now, by the time she’s 65, that $262,500
of her original money has ballooned to $1,277,158.92.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.090
Nice job Betty!

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.680
A couple things to keep in mind with this
scenario.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.400
It’s very likely that goods and services
will cost more in the future due to inflation.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.600
However, it’s also very likely that a 30
year old like Betty will see her salary increase

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.280
as she gains more experience and skill.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.570
If she sticks to that same 15% of her salary,
she can expect to have even more set aside

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:06.570
for retirement.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.090
What if you’re older than Betty and getting
a late start?

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:15.070
Well, that may mean that you need to set aside
more of your paycheck, say 20 or 25 percent.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.310
Or you may have to wait until your 70s to
retire.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:23.260
Either of these options is better than doing
nothing or counting on winning the lottery.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.200
There are many other factors that can change
your specific situation.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.360
Inheritances, social security, pensions, medical
conditions.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.390
If you’re not sure where to begin, you can
seek out the help of a financial planner who

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.270
is a sworn fiduciary.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.530
They can outline a plan that fits your needs
and show you that preparing for retirement

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:43.840
is not as intimidating as you may think.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.810
You don’t have to be into shuffleboard or
bird-watching to expect a little time off

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.340
in your golden years.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.110
And some people want to work as long as they
can.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:56.460
But everyone wants the power to decide that
for themselves, especially after a lifetime

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:57.460
of hard work.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.120
Of course, if you do manage to get access
to a time machine, you can always fall back

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.400
on the old “Sports Almanac Retirement Plan.”

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.420
And that’s our two cents!

